      "message": "For the cases where the system is in S3/S5.\n\n1. bat_level \u003e BAT_LEVEL_PD_LIMIT\n   a. pd_limit_en \u003d 0 at beginning of the function\n      \u003d\u003e !pd_limit_en \u0026\u0026 (bat_level \u003e BAT_LEVEL_PD_LIMIT) is true, so\n         pd_limit_5v(1);\n   b. pd_limit_en \u003d 1 at beginning of the function\n      \u003d\u003e None of the tests are true, so\n         pd_limit_5v(1);\n\n2. bat_level \u003c BAT_LEVEL_PD_LIMIT\n   a. pd_limit_en \u003d 0 at beginning of the function\n      \u003d\u003e None of the tests are true, so\n         pd_limit_5v(0);\n   b. pd_limit_en \u003d 1 at beginning of the function\n      \u003d\u003e pd_limit_en \u0026\u0026 (bat_level \u003c BAT_LEVEL_PD_LIMIT) is true, so\n         pd_limit_5v(0);\n\n(btw, there is some hysteresis when bat_level \u003d\u003d BAT_LEVEL_PD_LIMIT, is that desired? I don\u0027t think bat_level fluctuates a lot, so this should not be required?)\n\nFor the cases where the system is in S0:\n  - pd_limited_en is set to 0, no matter it\u0027s previous state.\n\nSo basically you don\u0027t need pd_limit_en to be static (the result is the same in all cases).\n\nI think a simple expression like this, matching the comment above the function, should do it:\n\npd_limit_en \u003d chipset_in_state(CHIPSET_STATE_ANY_OFF |\n\t\t\t      CHIPSET_STATE_ANY_SUSPEND) \u0026\u0026 bat_level \u003e BAT_LEVEL_PD_LIMIT;",
